    "description": "Fulfillment Service uses one asynchronous HTTP client with 120 connections for Inventory, Shipping Rates, and Address Validation. Inventory began accepting connections but never responds, so its 60 in-flight requests consume half of the shared pool and fill its queue. Shipping and address requests then fail even though their dependencies are healthy. The service has a fixed concurrency budget and must not create more threads or connections during the incident. Each dependency has different latency and business impact, and inventory traffic must not starve shipping quotes needed for checkout. We need an explicit partitioning rule that reserves independent connection and worker capacity for each failure domain, while documenting that it protects only the client side and does not remove shared network or provider limits.",
    "decision_statement": "Decide how Fulfillment Service should partition its finite client capacity so a hanging inventory dependency cannot consume resources needed by healthy dependencies.",
    "notes": "Bulkhead fits partitioning the finite client into independent per-dependency capacity; circuit-breaker and timeout are near-misses (stop calling the hanging dependency, bound how long stuck calls hold slots), supported by fail-fast rejection on partition saturation. Replaced the rate-limiting sibling, which cannot contain concurrency held by a never-responding dependency, with timeout moved from supporting; reworded time out to fail as a defensive de-leak."
  },
